 "title": "qué pasa, e → ie stem change, pensar, entender, perder",
 "cards": [
  {
   "title": "pasar = to happen",
   "body": "Pasar also means 'to happen' (to pass in the sense of occur). ¿Qué pasó? (stress on the end) is the past, 'what happened?', which comes later.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "What's happening? / What's going on?",
     "es": "¿Qué pasa?"
    },
    {
     "en": "I want to know what's going on.",
     "es": "Quiero saber qué pasa."
    }
   ],
   "t": 0
  },
  {
   "title": "Stressed e splits into ie",
   "body": "When an e in the verb's root (not its ending) gets the stress, it often splits into ie: querer → quiero, tener → tiene, venir → viene. Tengo and vengo keep the e, possibly because the g 'holds it up'.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "to want → I want",
     "es": "querer → quiero"
    },
    {
     "en": "to have → he has",
     "es": "tener → tiene"
    }
   ],
   "caveat": "Most of the time, not always: llego and espero don't change.",
   "t": 73.0
  },
  {
   "title": "You can see it between languages",
   "body": "English 'tempo' vs Spanish tiempo (time). The same split appears in other related words.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "time",
     "es": "tiempo"
    },
    {
     "en": "concert",
     "es": "concierto"
    },
    {
     "en": "November",
     "es": "noviembre"
    }
   ],
   "t": 211.1
  },
  {
   "title": "pensar = to think",
   "body": "Decide what type of word it is first (a verb), then check its type (pensar: -ar). That stops 'piense' vowel harmony: it's piensa.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I think",
     "es": "pienso"
    },
    {
     "en": "he / she thinks",
     "es": "piensa"
    },
    {
     "en": "What do you think?",
     "es": "¿Qué piensas?"
    }
   ],
   "t": 290.5
  },
  {
   "title": "pienso + verb = I plan to",
   "body": "'I think to eat' means 'I plan on eating'.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I plan on eating later.",
     "es": "Pienso comer más tarde."
    }
   ],
   "t": 389.9
  },
  {
   "title": "entender = to understand",
   "body": "Comprender (like 'comprehend') also works and doesn't split, but entender is more common, and it splits.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I understand",
     "es": "entiendo"
    },
    {
     "en": "he understands / they understand",
     "es": "entiende / entienden"
    }
   ],
   "t": 459.0
  },
  {
   "title": "perder = to lose",
   "body": "Related to 'perish' and 'perishable'. Perder → pierdo.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I lose it.",
     "es": "Lo pierdo."
    },
    {
     "en": "I lose them.",
     "es": "Los pierdo."
    }
   ],
   "t": 506.2
  }
 ],
